---
name: open-webui
partof: llm
desc: Chat UI for Ollama
port: 8080
healthz: "/health"
# Open WebUI's subpath support is partial (WebSocket + static asset
# paths have historically been finicky), so it gets a dedicated host
# port like vaultwarden/qbittorrent rather than path-routing under
# the main FQDN. Remember to keep services/caddy/service.yml.elp's
# `ports:` list in sync (8002:8002).
public_port: 8002
public_path: /
user: root  # image runs as its own internal user; SQLite under /app/backend/data
docker_config:
  image: ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:main
  environment:
  - OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434
  - WEBUI_URL=https://<%= hostname %>:8002
  # Must stay `true` so api-config's first-deploy signup succeeds — Open
  # WebUI returns 403 to *every* call when ENABLE_SIGNUP=false, even the
  # very first one (no "first user bypass" exists). Safe to leave on
  # permanently: after the first admin exists, Open WebUI itself returns
  # 403 to any further public signup regardless of this flag.
  - ENABLE_SIGNUP=true
  # Trusted-header SSO: Authelia authenticates the human, caddy's
  # forward_auth copies Remote-Email/Remote-Name onto the proxied
  # request (services/caddy/Caddyfile.elp), and Open WebUI trusts them
  # — auto-provisioning/logging-in that user with NO separate OWUI
  # login. api-config reaches OWUI directly on mediaserver-network
  # (http://open-webui:8080, not via caddy), carries no Remote-* header,
  # so it still authenticates its signin/bootstrap flow with
  # admin_email/admin_password — that account stays as the M2M service
  # account (it is NOT removed; only the *human* separate login is).
  # Caveat: OWUI makes the first-ever user admin. On the existing
  # persistent DB the api-config admin already exists, so humans
  # arriving via SSO are regular users. On a *fresh* DB there's an
  # order race (api-config bootstrap vs. a human browsing first) —
  # DR-only, documented in services/open-webui/README.md.
  - WEBUI_AUTH_TRUSTED_EMAIL_HEADER=Remote-Email
  - WEBUI_AUTH_TRUSTED_NAME_HEADER=Remote-Name
  volumes:
  - "<%= install_base %>/config/open-webui:/app/backend/data"
  restart: unless-stopped
api_config:
  # Auth endpoints live under /api/v1/auths/* — same /api/<api_version>
  # base the upserts/posts machinery uses. The steps-only flow below
  # writes full paths, so api_version is only documentary here.
  api_version: v1
# Literal block — ELP tags inside resolve at stage 2.
#
# Flow on each deploy:
#   1. signin — captures $token. Succeeds if admin already exists.
#   2. signup — captures $token. Succeeds on first deploy when admin doesn't
#      exist yet; 400s afterwards (already exists). Both paths leave $token
#      set; api-config substitutes it into the Authorization headers below.
#   3+. Register the grafana mcpo tool server + create a Grafana-Assistant
#      custom model with a primed system prompt. POSTs replace the stored
#      state, so they're idempotent — every deploy reconciles to this.
#      Manual additions in the UI to tool_servers or this model get
#      clobbered by re-deploy; that's intentional config-as-code, document
#      it in the README.
api_resources: |
  steps:
    - name: signin
      request:
        method: POST
        url: $base_url/api/v1/auths/signin
        json:
          email: "<%= (for-service :open-webui admin_email) %>"
          password: "<%= (for-service :open-webui admin_password) %>"
      extract:
        regex: '"token"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"'
        store_as: token
      success_when:
        status: 200
      on_failure: continue   # fall through to signup on first deploy
    - name: bootstrap-admin
      request:
        method: POST
        url: $base_url/api/v1/auths/signup
        json:
          name: "<%= (for-service :open-webui admin_username) %>"
          email: "<%= (for-service :open-webui admin_email) %>"
          password: "<%= (for-service :open-webui admin_password) %>"
          profile_image_url: "/user.png"
      extract:
        regex: '"token"\s*:\s*"([^"]+)"'
        store_as: token
      success_when:
        status: 200
      on_failure: continue   # benign 400 'user exists' on re-deploys
    - name: register-grafana-tool-server
      request:
        method: POST
        url: $base_url/api/v1/configs/tool_servers
        headers:
          Authorization: "Bearer $token"
        json:
          TOOL_SERVER_CONNECTIONS:
            - url: "https://<%= hostname %>:<%= (for-service :mcpo public_port) %>"
              path: "openapi.json"
              type: "openapi"
              auth_type: "none"
              key: ""
              config:
                enable: true
                function_name_filter_list: ""
                access_grants: []
              info:
                id: "grafana"
                name: "Grafana"
                description: "Query Grafana, Prometheus, and Loki via mcp-grafana"
    - name: delete-grafana-assistant-model
      # Upsert: /models/create 400s if the id exists and there is no
      # update endpoint we rely on, so delete-then-create makes the
      # model reconcile to this config every deploy. 404 on the first
      # deploy (nothing to delete) is expected — hence continue.
      request:
        # OWUI: POST (not DELETE → 405) /model/delete, id in the JSON
        # body (query param → 422 "body Field required"). Keep ?id= too
        # — harmless if ignored, covers OWUI versions that read query.
        method: POST
        url: $base_url/api/v1/models/model/delete?id=grafana-assistant
        headers:
          Authorization: "Bearer $token"
        json:
          id: grafana-assistant
      on_failure: continue
    - name: create-grafana-assistant-model
      request:
        method: POST
        url: $base_url/api/v1/models/create
        headers:
          Authorization: "Bearer $token"
        json:
          id: "grafana-assistant"
          base_model_id: "qwen2.5:3b"
          name: "Grafana Assistant"
          params:
            # Native function calling: pass the tool schema to ollama's
            # tools API so the model emits structured tool_calls. OWUI's
            # default mode prompt-injects a ReAct format that small
            # models (qwen2.5:3b) fail by echoing the example as text.
            function_calling: native
            system: |
              You are a monitoring assistant for a self-hosted media-server stack. You answer questions about service health and logs ONLY by calling the provided tools (Prometheus metrics, Loki logs, Grafana dashboards). You have no knowledge of live state without a tool result.

              ABSOLUTE RULES:
              - Never put a PromQL or LogQL query in your reply. A query in your message means you did the wrong thing — issue a tool call instead.
              - If no tool result has come back yet, you do not have an answer. Do not guess, predict, or describe what a query "would" return.
              - Only after a tool result returns do you answer, stating the concrete finding (actual metric value, which alerts are firing, matching log lines) in one or two sentences.

              For a service health question: use the Prometheus tool to check whether that service's `up` metric is 1 and whether any alerts are firing for it, and also check for any globally firing alerts (a Prometheus/Loki/disk alert can invalidate everything else). For a logs question: use the Loki tool to read that service's logs. To discover dashboards, use the Grafana dashboard search tool.

              KNOWN SERVICES (the `service` label in Prometheus and Loki):
              alertmanager, alloy, blackbox-exporter, cadvisor, grafana, jellyfin, loki, mcpo, open-webui, prometheus, prowlarr, qbittorrent, radarr, sonarr, vaultwarden

              If a tool returns nothing, broaden the query once before concluding. Keep replies concise.
            num_gpu: 0   # GTX 970M can't host 3B; force CPU. Drop when the host upgrades.
          meta:
            description: "Queries Grafana/Prometheus/Loki for service health and logs."
            profile_image_url: "/static/favicon.png"
            capabilities:
              tool_servers: true
          access_control: null
      on_failure: continue   # delete-step already cleared any prior; continue is defensive
order: 96  # after ollama (95) so the upstream is up when the UI boots

# Gated behind Authelia. Humans hit caddy → forward_auth → Authelia,
# then OWUI trusts the copied Remote-Email/Remote-Name (env above) so
# there's no second OWUI login. api-config reaches OWUI directly on
# mediaserver-network (not via caddy), so forward_auth never touches
# the reconcile. OWUI's own /api/* keeps its bearer auth (Authelia
# gates the page, OWUI gates its API) — no public_paths needed.
gateway_auth: true
